\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {Multi-timescale structure of behavior in a virtual patch-foraging task.} (\subref  {fig:1-a}) Left: head-fixed mice run on a wheel through a multi-sensory virtual odor patch foraging environment. Right: High, low, and unrewarded patch types are cued by odors, differ in initial reward probability, and depletes with each reward collected. (\subref  {fig:1-b}) Running velocity (black trace), stop choice tones, rewards, and odors (orange, green, or purple) for example patches in a session. (\subref  {fig:1-c}) Running velocity shows stereotyped stop actions at odor site entries and longer stop times in rewarded vs unrewarded sites. (\subref  {fig:1-d},\subref  {fig:1-e}) Mice foraged in two rewarded patch types, stopping more in the high value type, and consistently skipping the unrewarded patch type (across mice, n=24) (\subref  {fig:1-f}-\subref  {fig:1-t}) Behavior is organized across nested timescales: sites (sub-second), patches (tens of seconds to minutes), and sessions (tens of minutes). (\subref  {fig:1-g}-\subref  {fig:1-k}) Stereotyped action motifs at the odor site level. Velocity and sniffing in an odor site shaded by HMM states (\subref  {fig:1-g}) and as trajectory (\subref  {fig:1-h}). (\subref  {fig:1-i}--\subref  {fig:1-k}) HMM states (one row per site) and mean velocity aligned to odor site approach state (\subref  {fig:1-i}), site entry (\subref  {fig:1-j})and leaving (\subref  {fig:1-k}). (\subref  {fig:1-m}-\subref  {fig:1-p}) Action motifs shift as mice approach patch leaving decision. HMM states (\subref  {fig:1-m}) and velocity-sniff trajectories (\subref  {fig:1-n}) colored by sites from patch leaving. (\subref  {fig:1-o},\subref  {fig:1-p}) Mice enter odor sites with decreased sniff frequency, increased velocity, (\subref  {fig:1-o}), and slower reaction times (\subref  {fig:1-p}; mean $\pm $ s.e.m., $n=13$ mice). (\subref  {fig:1-r},\subref  {fig:1-t}) Session-averaged probability of three engagement states (engaged, disengage-run, disengage-stop; mean $\pm $ s.e.m. across sessions) for two mice, one disengaged by stopping (\subref  {fig:1-r}, $n=20$ sessions) the other disengaged by running (\subref  {fig:1-t}, $n=18$ sessions). (\subref  {fig:1-s}) Velocity and sniff frequency reflect engagement across time in an example session.\relax }}{2}{figure.caption.2}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:1}{{1}{2}{\textbf {Multi-timescale structure of behavior in a virtual patch-foraging task.} (\subref {fig:1-a}) Left: head-fixed mice run on a wheel through a multi-sensory virtual odor patch foraging environment. Right: High, low, and unrewarded patch types are cued by odors, differ in initial reward probability, and depletes with each reward collected. (\subref {fig:1-b}) Running velocity (black trace), stop choice tones, rewards, and odors (orange, green, or purple) for example patches in a session. (\subref {fig:1-c}) Running velocity shows stereotyped stop actions at odor site entries and longer stop times in rewarded vs unrewarded sites. (\subref {fig:1-d},\subref {fig:1-e}) Mice foraged in two rewarded patch types, stopping more in the high value type, and consistently skipping the unrewarded patch type (across mice, n=24) (\subref {fig:1-f}-\subref {fig:1-t}) Behavior is organized across nested timescales: sites (sub-second), patches (tens of seconds to minutes), and sessions (tens of minutes). (\subref {fig:1-g}-\subref {fig:1-k}) Stereotyped action motifs at the odor site level. Velocity and sniffing in an odor site shaded by HMM states (\subref {fig:1-g}) and as trajectory (\subref {fig:1-h}). (\subref {fig:1-i}--\subref {fig:1-k}) HMM states (one row per site) and mean velocity aligned to odor site approach state (\subref {fig:1-i}), site entry (\subref {fig:1-j})and leaving (\subref {fig:1-k}). (\subref {fig:1-m}-\subref {fig:1-p}) Action motifs shift as mice approach patch leaving decision. HMM states (\subref {fig:1-m}) and velocity-sniff trajectories (\subref {fig:1-n}) colored by sites from patch leaving. (\subref {fig:1-o},\subref {fig:1-p}) Mice enter odor sites with decreased sniff frequency, increased velocity, (\subref {fig:1-o}), and slower reaction times (\subref {fig:1-p}; mean $\pm $ s.e.m., $n=13$ mice). (\subref {fig:1-r},\subref {fig:1-t}) Session-averaged probability of three engagement states (engaged, disengage-run, disengage-stop; mean $\pm $ s.e.m. across sessions) for two mice, one disengaged by stopping (\subref {fig:1-r}, $n=20$ sessions) the other disengaged by running (\subref {fig:1-t}, $n=18$ sessions). (\subref {fig:1-s}) Velocity and sniff frequency reflect engagement across time in an example session.\relax }{figure.caption.2}{}}
